LOGBOOK.md:9:- IMPLEMENTATION: `SSHProfileSnapshotV1` now carries the accepted complete profile, canonical endpoint, credential reference/generation, ordered host-key policy, and trust history as immutable value types. A strict deterministic JSON pass enforces UTF-8, sorted keys, canonical strings/numbers, no insignificant whitespace, duplicate rejection, depth 8, exact v1 envelope headers, recursive prohibited-field names, and exhaustive field invariants before exposing the model.
LOGBOOK.md:10:- DECISION: The 4096-byte bound is checked over the actual binary-property-list representation of the exact three-key `providerConfiguration`, so owner and manager-contract metadata count toward the NetworkExtension envelope. The macOS adapter reads only `NETunnelProviderProtocol.providerConfiguration`; it has no App Group, Keychain, route, network, packet, or profile-write path.
LOGBOOK.md:11:- GENERATION SAFETY: A fresh `SSHProfileRuntimeSnapshotLoader` owns one runtime start. A present exact five-field v1 start request binds `configurationGeneration` to the SHA-256 digest of the exact stored canonical snapshot bytes before first capture; nil options may capture the validated stored value. Byte-identical rereads return the original capture, while stale requests, same-generation field replacement, newer-generation replacement, and concurrent competing captures fail with the fixed privacy-safe `profileGenerationMismatch` error.
LOGBOOK.md:12:- PRIVACY REWORK: The recursive field scan applies case/punctuation normalization plus a bounded prohibited-root/fixed-suffix policy. Nested fixtures cover `privateKeyMaterial`, `privateKeyData`, `passphraseBytes`, `passwordValue`, `seedBytes`, `decryptedPrivateKey`, `stagingCredentialPayload`, raw-host-key data, and key-byte payload variants.
LOGBOOK.md:22:- ROOT CAUSE: `neagent` previously received zero PlugInKit matches because both the development-signed host and embedded packet-tunnel provider omitted `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Adding App Sandbox to both exact targets made PlugInKit discover one provider at the expected embedded path after launch; Network Extension stayed exactly `packet-tunnel-provider`, with no App Groups or signed Keychain Sharing entitlement.
LOGBOOK.md:34:- SIGNING: The initial background process could select the approved profile/identity but could not use the login-Keychain private key (`errSecInternalComponent`). Running the same checked-in command through the approved Aqua Terminal seam resolved that execution-context issue without exporting a key or weakening policy. Both profile-scoped signing preflights and the signed archive then passed.
LOGBOOK.md:41:- PORTAL RESULT: Xcode automatic provisioning created or updated exactly the four r12 macOS identifiers and downloaded one device-bound Mac Team Provisioning Profile for each: `works.relux.tunnel.mac`, `.mac.tunnel`, `.probe.mac`, and `.probe.mac.tunnel`. Network Extensions is profile-authorized on hosts and providers alike; no portal reauthentication or two-factor challenge occurred.
LOGBOOK.md:43:- SCOPE EVIDENCE: Exactly four local provisioning profiles changed during the run and all four are the approved macOS development set; no unexpected, iOS, or distribution profile changed. The isolated harness declared no App Group, Keychain Sharing, iOS, distribution, or legacy-bundle mutation, and raw profiles/certificates were neither attached nor committed.
LOGBOOK.md:44:- ANOMALY: The portal/profile phase succeeded, but final local codesigning returned `errSecInternalComponent` for each harness target because unattended signing-key access was unavailable. A separate all-target no-sign compile succeeded. Read-only Safari automation also remained unavailable (timeout exit 142), consistent with APC34W-B5; neither issue caused a portal reauthentication challenge or invalidated the downloaded profiles.
LOGBOOK.md:50:- ACCESS ANOMALIES: Read-only Safari automation stalled and was terminated at exit 130 without page data. A named-profile `notarytool history` probe exited 1 because the default Keychain was locked; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns revalidation after unattended Keychain access is restored. No secret, credential, certificate private key, recovery code, or full device identifier was captured.
LOGBOOK.md:55:- PRIVACY POLICY: Credential values and secret paths remain prohibited in repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history. Safe command-name-only history does not fail the ceremony; secret-bearing arguments, credential input, identifiers, account credentials, and key bytes still do.
LOGBOOK.md:59:- EVIDENCE GAPS: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes demonstrate effective access but cannot prove the required Always Allow grants were performed in C1; an authenticated portal session does not prove two-factor completed during C1; two safe credential command-name lines in history mean literal per-command history suppression cannot be claimed. These facts cannot be repaired retrospectively by editing the outcome.
LOGBOOK.md:64:- AUTHORITY: The owner confirmed an authenticated Apple Developer session for Relux Works, LLC and authority for revision `2026-07-28.r12`: exactly the four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered. No App Group record/capability, Keychain access-group portal mutation, iOS App ID, distribution profile, or `works.relux.proxy` change is authorized.
LOGBOOK.md:65:- CUSTODY: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes succeeded for the named Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities; the named `relux-works-notary` profile is in the login Keychain; the owner retained the source notarization private-key file as owner-controlled recovery material outside repo/board/logs; Sparkle 2.9.4 generated the EdDSA private key into login-Keychain custody.
LOGBOOK.md:66:- PRIVACY: Targeted post-ceremony scans found zero secret/key-path/key-ID/issuer-ID/account-credential candidate files in the Git-scoped repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history, and zero key files in repository/board scope. History nevertheless contains one safe Sparkle generation command-name line and one safe Keychain-unlock command-name line, with no password flags; full history suppression therefore cannot be claimed.
LOGBOOK.md:67:- ANOMALY: The older `TASK-260728-3a2dnr_ceremony-c1.md` planning artifact predates matrix rule G4/K2 and still describes two macOS IDs plus App Group/Keychain grants. It is historical and must not drive execution; the live task contract and accepted r12 matrix are authoritative.
LOGBOOK.md:70:- CONFORMANCE: A live OpenSSH fixture now authenticates an in-memory Ed25519 credential through the adapter's async `SSHPublicKeyCredential.sign` callback, records an actual opaque-signer invocation, preserves host-policy-before-credential ordering, carries only a Keychain-shaped credential reference, runs exec, and returns every owned resource to zero. The existing P-256 deployed-fallback coverage remains intact.
LOGBOOK.md:77:- GAP: The revised M0 contract requires noninteractive Ed25519 plus an approved fallback through the opaque external signer. The live adapter fixture authenticates only with an in-memory P-256 signer; the pinned C rekey probe uses file-based Ed25519 auth and therefore does not exercise the adapter/Keychain signer boundary. A scoped search for an Ed25519 live-auth credential in `LibSSH2AdapterIntegrationTests.swift` exits 1.
LOGBOOK.md:106:- ROOT CAUSE: The generic timeout helper selected the deadline result but then drained its structured task group, so a resolver, connector, host policy, credential provider, or upload source that ignored task cancellation could hold the public operation and transport close beyond their configured deadlines.
LOGBOOK.md:119:- ROOT CAUSE: Long-lived reads reused the write-credit deadline; the authentication timeout raced a waiter that still joined an independently owned signer; rekey and keepalive deadlines began only after the shared session gate; and canceled upload cleanup inherited task cancellation, causing its cleanup progress handler to close the shared TCP bridge.
LOGBOOK.md:125:- ROOT CAUSE: Channel read/write timeout cancellation shut down the shared socket bridge; channel opens rejected `rekeying` before entering the session gate and started their deadline only after gate admission; each rekey caller owned the connection-global KEX; and concurrent writes could resume libssh2's single per-channel write state with a different payload after `EAGAIN`. Credential results were checked against factory capabilities but not the caller-provided allowlist.
LOGBOOK.md:126:- REWORK: Socket-service cancellation now retires only its independently cancellable readiness operation. Channel opens may queue behind rekey under their original bounded deadline. One bounded shared rekey flight coalesces trigger reasons and lets a canceled caller detach while KEX continues. Per-channel FIFO gates retain exact write arguments across `EAGAIN`, with queued bytes still reserved against the caller cap. Returned credential algorithms must belong to both adapter capabilities and the request allowlist.
LOGBOOK.md:127:- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic loopback regressions prove canceled and timed-out reads leave sibling channels and the session ready; an open times out while rekey is deliberately suspended and a second queued open succeeds afterward; two rekey triggers produce one KEX while the first caller cancels; four concurrent same-channel payloads survive forced bridge backpressure byte-exactly; and a supported-but-disallowed credential algorithm fails before authentication.
LOGBOOK.md:133:- CONFORMANCE: A serialized macOS loopback fixture starts ephemeral OpenSSH with an in-memory P-256 external signer and proves host-policy-before-credential ordering, approved public-key authentication, direct-tcpip echo, concurrent exec opens, exec/stdin upload, server-rekey traffic survival, manual client rekey, keepalive transmission, five connected read/cancel races, and three successful connect/close resource baselines. The four M3 semantics remain explicit `unsupported` reports owned by `TASK-260728-3cveay`.
LOGBOOK.md:144:- IMPLEMENTATION: `ReluxTunnelLibSSH2Adapter` is the sole Swift target importing the pinned static libssh2/OpenSSL XCFramework. A serialized transport actor drives custom send/receive callbacks in nonblocking mode over the injected `SSHTCPConnection`; bounded async bridges turn socket and external-Keychain-signer progress into `LIBSSH2_ERROR_EAGAIN` without blocking a provider executor.
LOGBOOK.md:145:- SECURITY: The adapter applies explicit KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC allowlists with no SHA-1/CBC fallback, delivers the exact raw host-key blob plus negotiated host-key method and canonical SHA-256 fingerprint to policy before credential lookup, and carries only opaque credential references/public signing material across Core. Engine prose and endpoint/user/command data never enter errors or typed diagnostics.
LOGBOOK.md:152:- IMPLEMENTATION: `SSHDeferredSemanticReport` exposes `reported`, `notReported`, and `unsupported`. Exact receive-credit policy/snapshots, open reasons, exit metadata, KEX generation, window/server-rekey/keepalive metrics, and manual keepalive RTT now require one of those states; no default zero/false or mandatory full-fidelity policy remains. Host-before-auth, approved public-key auth, direct-tcpip, exec/stdin upload, client and server rekey handling, bounded buffers/backpressure, cancellation/lifecycle, Keychain-only credential references, keepalive transmission, available metrics, and privacy-safe errors remain mandatory.
LOGBOOK.md:181:- ROOT CAUSE: r10 pre-authorized exactly one conditional exception (`X1-C1`) and gate R39 established it by requiring the register entry and its mirror on the target row to **agree**. That is a consistency check. Verdict 09 moved **both copies** of three fields and `validate-matrix.py` exited **0** each time, where a negative control must exit 1: the path widened `/Library/Keychains/` → `/`, `developer-id` vanished from `channelsIfArmed`, and `armedBy` moved to `TASK-260715-9yp8to`. Reproduced against the unmodified r10 artifact **with the r10 validator and r10 rationale** before the finding was accepted — judging old data with the new gate proves nothing. CONFIRMED.
LOGBOOK.md:195:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:198:- FINDING: installed products settle it in **neither** direction. Re-read on this Mac: Surfshark ships the `/Library/Keychains/` exception at `LSMinimumSystemVersion` **10.15**, bundled with `/private/var/db/mds/` and a third path; PureVPN's two providers carry `keychain-access-groups` at **10.15**; Tailscale's carries neither at **12.4**. Support ranges that wide isolate nothing about macOS 15.0. K1's clause runs **both ways** — a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.
LOGBOOK.md:200:- DECISION: rule **X1** gains a `conditionalExceptions` register — **reviewed but not granted** — holding pre-authorized entry **X1-C1** (`…temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write` = `/Library/Keychains/`, `macos.provider`, both macOS channels, `armed: false`). It is **disjoint from the active register by construction**, because R26's equality (active register == the exceptions the rows actually author) is what keeps the active one honest. Rationale: the spec bans an *unreviewed* exception, so one a supported OS may require must be reviewed **before** the evidence — otherwise X1 rejects the right answer the day it arrives.
LOGBOOK.md:202:- DECISION: owner is **`TASK-260715-1uxx3i`** — an **existing** task. Its `macos-15` minimum lane already exists in the deployment-target policy and reading the runner's own `application.sb` is a credential-free static read inside its AC2; it already reaches this contract transitively via `TASK-260715-uyju7n`, so **no board element was created** and no edge added. `TASK-260715-1o9wjz` consumes the same answer at runtime. Tracked as **OC-5**.
LOGBOOK.md:212:- ROOT CAUSE: the matrix has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1 and it was **never compared against the board's dependency graph**. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: 19 declared consumers, **16 reachable, 3 not** — `TASK-260715-1o9wjz`, `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`, `TASK-260715-29ws8l`. With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run all three while the matrix sat in `analysis` under its **eighth** changes-requested verdict, authoring the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract against an unaccepted contract.
LOGBOOK.md:215:- FIX: one board edge — `link(TASK-260715-29ws8l, blocked_by=TASK-260715-ypo7yo)`, cycle-checked over the whole graph first. It orders all three because 29ws8l already blocks the other two, and it is semantically real: 29ws8l **AC2** specifies "App Group and `providerConfiguration` data" and **AC4** specifies "Keychain accessibility, access group" — exactly what rules G4 and K2 decide. Authoring it without this matrix reproduces the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.
LOGBOOK.md:231:- FINDING (**third instance, unreported by any verdict**): registering A6's scope required its clauses to render in the rationale document — which still carried the **r4** contract in its assertion table, granting `macos.host` the Keychain group **r5 removed under rule K2**. Identical to the verdict-05 F1 defect, one revision later and one file over. **R33 could not see it because R33 renders `grantClause`s, not assertions.** The table also had **no rows at all** for A17 and A18. Recorded in `S2.staleClaimsFound`; R35's rationale-document check closes that half.
LOGBOOK.md:257:- ROOT CAUSE: r5 moved eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left `crossPlatformRules[2]` still asserting the **r4** contract — the shared Keychain access group granted to `macos.host`. The r5 validator passed **846 checks** because not one of its 31 rules looked at the summary. The generalisable defect is not the sentence: it is that a summary *asserting* a grant is a **second, unsynchronised statement** of data that lives elsewhere, and nothing in a per-row validator can see the contradiction. Reviewer verdict 05 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:262:- DECISION: the corrected Keychain sentence states the exclusion **universally** ("to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family") rather than naming the macOS host as excluded, which is a deliberate divergence from the reviewer's literal rework text. **Naming a target is what went stale**: r4 named the host as a grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all eight rows and has nothing to forget. Recorded in the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained difference.
LOGBOOK.md:269:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets**. Rule **K2**: granted only where a **named second target** in the contract reads the same Data Protection Keychain item. Sharing is the entitlement's *only* function — it does not unlock the DPK, change durability, or survive anything the default group does not — so any argument for it that cannot name a second reader is an argument for something it does not do. On macOS there is no second reader: K1 excludes the root provider, and neither probe touches the DPK.
LOGBOOK.md:277:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
LOGBOOK.md:293:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
LOGBOOK.md:295:- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
LOGBOOK.md:296:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
LOGBOOK.md:297:- ANOMALY: `Security.framework/Headers/SecKeychain.h` is **ISO-8859 encoded**. A plain UTF-8 `grep` over it silently prints nothing and exits 1 — indistinguishable from "the symbol isn't there". This nearly produced the opposite error, concluding the domain APIs had been removed from the SDK. `iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8` first when grepping Apple headers; an empty grep result on a header is not evidence of absence.
LOGBOOK.md:300:### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
LOGBOOK.md:301:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:302:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
LOGBOOK.md:303:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
LOGBOOK.md:304:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
LOGBOOK.md:305:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
LOGBOOK.md:307:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
LOGBOOK.md:308:- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
LOGBOOK.md:332:- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
LOGBOOK.md:333:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
LOGBOOK.md:341:### 0657 — The macOS provider runs as root, so the specified Keychain sharing cannot work (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
LOGBOOK.md:342:- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
LOGBOOK.md:344:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
LOGBOOK.md:345:- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
LOGBOOK.md:373:- DECISION: `TASK-260715-39xz9g` is no longer a conditional hold. A read-only `BatchMode` probe against the owner-authorized alias authenticated without a prompt (remote reports Darwin); no hostname, address, username, key path, or credential was recorded. The probe is explicitly *not* conformance evidence — raw pre-auth host-key evidence is still owed.
LOGBOOK.md:381:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:382:- FINDING: The notarization credential was described as "available" while existing only as a mode-0600 `.p8` file — the project invariant is Keychain-only. ADR-025 now requires a named `notarytool` Keychain profile, a real authentication check, and a recorded disposition for the source file; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns it and blocks `3gkwn0`.
LOGBOOK.md:389:> Superseded by the 0655 entry: the 202-task claim, the single-session Ceremony C1, and the "notary credentials exist" readiness statement were all corrected.
LOGBOOK.md:396:- VERIFICATION: `task-board validate` → valid, 0 issues (was 46). Independent cycle traversal → 0. No source tree change: `git status --porcelain -- Sources Tests Protocol relay scripts config Package.swift Makefile` is empty. Invariant scan confirms host-key-before-auth, Keychain-only secrets, fail-closed DNS, bounded memory, public packetFlow bridge, rootless exec/stdio relay, and signed/notarized release all survive the edit. Evidence: `.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/`.
LOGBOOK.md:460:- FINDING: The seed threat model stated several accepted designs as shipped mitigations even though the repository has no concrete packet-tunnel provider target, selected production SSH adapter, Keychain vault, DNS/route composition, relay deployment/frame-pump composition, degraded-mode controller, platform fail-closed implementation, Sparkle path, or application release pipeline.
LOGBOOK.md:463:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
LOGBOOK.md:476:### 1921 — Portable relay builds use one pinned, offline, credential-isolated toolchain (TASK-260715-27uz4n)
LOGBOOK.md:478:- SECURITY: Target builds construct an environment allowlist and isolate `HOME`, `TMPDIR`, `GOCACHE`, `GOMODCACHE`, and `GOPATH` per target below `.build/relay/work`; `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `GOPROXY=off`, `GOSUMDB=off`, `GOVCS=off`, `GOENV=off`, and `GOWORK=off` prevent undeclared downloads or workstation credential/config reuse.
LOGBOOK.md:579:- IMPLEMENTATION: The authoritative standard-library-only relay module now pins Go 1.26.5 and builds `relux-relay` plus `relux-relay-protocol-test` for Darwin/Linux amd64/arm64. Both expose deterministic credential-free metadata smoke output; the protocol-test entry point runs only empty health and one generated-schema-backed version mismatch, and the relay explicitly reports that feature behavior is not implemented.
LOGBOOK.md:585:- PRIVACY: The board runner's automatically attached raw spawn log was removed from the versionable outcome resources. Persistent evidence contains no raw process log, host identifier, absolute local path, credential, payload, or remote-controlled string.
LOGBOOK.md:615:- DIAGNOSTICS: Runtime schema v1 is additively extended to 139 fixed counters, 75 fixed gauges, and two 12-bucket cumulative histograms. Registry-owned current gauges publish through a generation-scoped fixed-size atomic coalescer and reconcile into snapshots independently of the bounded event lane, so dropped counter/histogram ingestion remains observable without leaving phantom flow/open/channel/byte gauges. The TCP surface contains only aggregate pressure/terminal tokens, counts, bytes, health, and channel-open latency; it accepts no destination, hostname, address, port, payload, credential, flow/channel ID, or free-form label. Maximum populated encoding is 13,568 bytes of the 65,536-byte limit and generation advancement isolates stale publications.
LOGBOOK.md:625:- CORRECTION: The normative ownership sequence now matches `HEVSOCKSBoundary.swift:284-299`: immediately after TCP accept/socket setup, an already-stopped or generation-retired boundary or a full pending-authentication set closes the descriptor without a SOCKS5 method reply, RFC 1929 reply, authentication enqueue, or adapter handoff.
LOGBOOK.md:626:- BOUNDARY: Wrong/stale capability and the M1 monotonic accept-to-auth deadline remain authentication-phase failures with only phase-and-remaining-budget-safe replies. The post-handoff adapter resource reservation is separate from the boundary pending-authentication ceiling.
LOGBOOK.md:631:- CORRECTION: Current `HEVSOCKSBoundary` uses `SO_RCVTIMEO`, which is a per-receive inactivity bound and can restart as bytes arrive; authentication sends have no deadline. The pending set is count-bounded, but current source does not prove an absolute accept-to-auth deadline. M1 must compute one monotonic deadline at accept, apply its remaining budget across greeting/auth reads, comparisons, and replies without progress extension, and prove slow-trickle/reply-stall eviction plus slot/descriptor release on iOS and macOS.
LOGBOOK.md:633:- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
LOGBOOK.md:637:- FIX: Both iOS and macOS `VPNHostSession` adapters now catch synchronous `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` errors and translate them through the existing `VPNPreferencePlatformError` model before crossing into `VPNSessionController`. Public `NEVPNError.configurationInvalid`, `.configurationDisabled`, and `.connectionFailed` retain their stable results; every other `NSError` retains its original domain and integer code.
LOGBOOK.md:644:- REGRESSION: `IOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` and `MacOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` forward the public `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` thrown `NSError` unchanged, while `VPNSessionController.performStart` maps only `VPNPreferencePlatformError` / `VPNPlatformError` and otherwise erases the real domain/code to reflected type plus code 0. The unit test injects a pre-normalized fake error, so the production adapter path is untested and cannot satisfy the accepted exact synchronous `NEVPNError` mapping.
LOGBOOK.md:675:- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
LOGBOOK.md:689:- RESIDUAL: Exact four-target bundle IDs, App Group, Keychain group, and entitlements are not yet accepted (`TASK-260715-ypo7yo`). Shared implementation proceeds through injected identities, while production composition fails closed instead of inventing values. Human ratification stays decoupled in `TASK-260717-1dsqnj`.
LOGBOOK.md:726:- PRIVACY: The default schema has no lane/run/flow IDs, DNS or destination fields, addresses, credentials, commands/stdin, payload content, or traffic samples. State transition times are monotonic process-uptime milliseconds; errors retain only validated domain/code; flow, memory, route, health, packet/byte, drop-reason, DNS-result, and SSH values are aggregates.
LOGBOOK.md:739:- SECURITY: The prior `TunnelConfiguration.parameters: [String:String]` escape hatch made private-key/passphrase exclusion structurally impossible. It was removed; provider/start configuration now contains only a schema-v1 UUID-backed profile reference, while configuration snapshots use distinct UUID-backed profile revision, credential, and trust references. Harness experiment parameters remain in harness-only recorded output and no longer cross into the runtime configuration.
LOGBOOK.md:951:- BLOCKER: The neutral credential is an opaque async signer (`SSHPublicKeyCredential`), but ReluxNIOSSH public-key auth accepts only concrete `NIOSSHPrivateKey` and signs synchronously inside an internal message initializer. The adapter cannot authenticate without a candidate downcast/private-key export or a new external-signer fork API.
LOGBOOK.md:979:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
LOGBOOK.md:1005:- DECISION: HEV's upstream SOCKS connection is restricted to an IPv4-loopback listener with fresh per-run RFC 1929 credentials. Only an authenticated owned channel reaches the injected adapter; pending-authentication count and timeout are caller inputs, and an external no-auth ingress test is rejected before the seam.
LOGBOOK.md:1008:- ANOMALY: Darwin accepted sockets inherited nonblocking behavior during the split RFC 1929 exchange; explicitly restoring blocking mode fixed a deterministic transient-`EAGAIN` authentication rejection without retries or sleeps.
