- PORTAL RESULT: Xcode automatic provisioning created or updated exactly the four r12 macOS identifiers and downloaded one device-bound Mac Team Provisioning Profile for each: `works.relux.tunnel.mac`, `.mac.tunnel`, `.probe.mac`, and `.probe.mac.tunnel`. Network Extensions is profile-authorized on hosts and providers alike; no portal reauthentication or two-factor challenge occurred.
- VALIDATION: All four profiles have team/prefix `262RZ595FP`, the exact application identifier, platform `OSX`, only the current Mac (`sha256:8ea3983a9990`), current validity through 2027-08-10, and unsuffixed `packet-tunnel-provider` without the Developer ID suffix. `com.apple.security.application-groups` is absent. The profile-only `keychain-access-groups = [262RZ595FP.*]` wildcard is expected under rule K2 and is not a finding.
- SCOPE EVIDENCE: Exactly four local provisioning profiles changed during the run and all four are the approved macOS development set; no unexpected, iOS, or distribution profile changed. The isolated harness declared no App Group, Keychain Sharing, iOS, distribution, or legacy-bundle mutation, and raw profiles/certificates were neither attached nor committed.
- ANOMALY: The portal/profile phase succeeded, but final local codesigning returned `errSecInternalComponent` for each harness target because unattended signing-key access was unavailable. A separate all-target no-sign compile succeeded. Read-only Safari automation also remained unavailable (timeout exit 142), consistent with APC34W-B5; neither issue caused a portal reauthentication challenge or invalidated the downloaded profiles.

### 2314 — Gate P0 Apple-account audit separates portal access from organization eligibility (TASK-260715-apc34w)
- TECHNICAL EVIDENCE: Ceremony C1 records an authenticated Relux Works, LLC portal/team session and authority for matrix `2026-07-28.r12`. A team-scoped Xcode capability bundle refreshed on 2026-08-10 reports Network Extensions as public and editable for development and Developer ID distribution; local signing inventory includes the Relux Works Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities.
- READINESS GAPS: The audit could not independently retrieve the Apple Developer membership enrollment type, active paid-through date, current Apple Developer Program License Agreement status, the operator's exact Account Holder/Admin role plus Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles grant, or a portal record proving that the current Apple-silicon Mac is registered. The Relux Works Account Holder owns membership/agreement proof; the Apple Platform/CI Admin and `TASK-260715-3jloqy` own role confirmation and Mac registration/profile evidence.
- DEVICE POLICY: The locally available Mac is recorded only by a truncated SHA-256 reference, model class, OS, and architecture; no full device identifier is persisted. The physical-iPhone row remains the named ADR-024 deferred gap owned by `TASK-260715-1kntdx`, neither pass nor failure and not a macOS Gate P0 blocker.
- ACCESS ANOMALIES: Read-only Safari automation stalled and was terminated at exit 130 without page data. A named-profile `notarytool history` probe exited 1 because the default Keychain was locked; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns revalidation after unattended Keychain access is restored. No secret, credential, certificate private key, recovery code, or full device identifier was captured.

### 2255 — Owner selects resumed functional evidence for Ceremony C1 (TASK-260728-q5kjta)
- OWNER DECISION: Option B from reviewer verdict 01 is accepted. ADR-028 and the C1 contract now distinguish one human-input board node from one continuous human sitting; the resumed 2026-07-28/2026-08-10 interactions remain one ceremony node, with the intervening producer/reviewer cycle stated rather than concealed.
- EVIDENCE POLICY: Effective prompt-free signing through the named identities/tools and authenticated Relux Works portal/team authority replace retrospective proof of the historical Always Allow click and separately timestamped two-factor completion. Missing interaction boundaries remain missing; no uninterrupted-session claim is allowed.
- PRIVACY POLICY: Credential values and secret paths remain prohibited in repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history. Safe command-name-only history does not fail the ceremony; secret-bearing arguments, credential input, identifiers, account credentials, and key bytes still do.

### 2234 — Ceremony C1 review stops on non-retroactive human evidence (TASK-260728-q5kjta)
- REVIEW VERDICT: BLOCKED. The privacy-safe record is honest, the r12 matrix and live-consumer gates pass, and the targeted leak scan is clean, but the record itself says the ceremony resumed across 2026-07-28 and 2026-08-10 with producer/reviewer work between interactions and without captured interaction start/end times. That directly fails literal AC1.
- EVIDENCE GAPS: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes demonstrate effective access but cannot prove the required Always Allow grants were performed in C1; an authenticated portal session does not prove two-factor completed during C1; two safe credential command-name lines in history mean literal per-command history suppression cannot be claimed. These facts cannot be repaired retrospectively by editing the outcome.
- RESOLUTION: Recommended path is one newly timed, uninterrupted owner sitting that idempotently repeats/confirms every in-scope grant, captures privacy-safe contemporaneous facts, and runs with history/echo suppression. Alternative is an explicit owner architecture/product decision to amend AC1/AC2/AC3/AC7 and ADR-028 to accept resumed functional evidence; accepting the current record under the unchanged contract is not valid.

### 2228 — Ceremony C1 resumed honestly under the accepted r12 macOS-only authority (TASK-260728-q5kjta)
- DEVIATION: C1 began on 2026-07-28 and owner confirmation resumed on 2026-08-10; exact human-interaction start/end times were not captured, and producer/reviewer work occurred between the interactions. The record must not claim one uninterrupted sitting or literal AC1 timing compliance.
- AUTHORITY: The owner confirmed an authenticated Apple Developer session for Relux Works, LLC and authority for revision `2026-07-28.r12`: exactly the four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered. No App Group record/capability, Keychain access-group portal mutation, iOS App ID, distribution profile, or `works.relux.proxy` change is authorized.
- CUSTODY: Prompt-free `/usr/bin/codesign` probes succeeded for the named Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities; the named `relux-works-notary` profile is in the login Keychain; the owner retained the source notarization private-key file as owner-controlled recovery material outside repo/board/logs; Sparkle 2.9.4 generated the EdDSA private key into login-Keychain custody.
- PRIVACY: Targeted post-ceremony scans found zero secret/key-path/key-ID/issuer-ID/account-credential candidate files in the Git-scoped repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history, and zero key files in repository/board scope. History nevertheless contains one safe Sparkle generation command-name line and one safe Keychain-unlock command-name line, with no password flags; full history suppression therefore cannot be claimed.
- ANOMALY: The older `TASK-260728-3a2dnr_ceremony-c1.md` planning artifact predates matrix rule G4/K2 and still describes two macOS IDs plus App Group/Keychain grants. It is historical and must not drive execution; the live task contract and accepted r12 matrix are authoritative.

### 2207 — libssh2 live evidence closes Ed25519 and automatic-rekey review gaps (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- CONFORMANCE: A live OpenSSH fixture now authenticates an in-memory Ed25519 credential through the adapter's async `SSHPublicKeyCredential.sign` callback, records an actual opaque-signer invocation, preserves host-policy-before-credential ordering, carries only a Keychain-shaped credential reference, runs exec, and returns every owned resource to zero. The existing P-256 deployed-fallback coverage remains intact.
- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic byte-threshold and injected-clock time-threshold tests drive the production libssh2 KEX path with an open channel, assert typed trigger/success events and `clientByteRekeys`/`clientTimeRekeys`, prove post-KEX traffic, and restore sessions, channels, sockets, tasks, allocations, and buffers to baseline after cancel/close.
- FINDING: A full-duplex echo can legitimately cross the per-direction byte threshold twice—once for sent protected bytes and again for received protected bytes. The byte-threshold regression therefore uses bounded one-directional exec/stdin traffic to prove one exact trigger without weakening the per-direction policy or hiding a second valid KEX.
- VERIFICATION: Five focused byte-trigger stress runs and three consecutive complete `swift test --filter LibSSH2` runs pass (44 tests/3 suites each). `make validate-core` passes 378 tests/31 suites plus boundary/build checks; pinned libssh2/rekey, source-tamper, dual-architecture macOS provider/harness, strict Swift format, and diff gates all exit 0. Board validation exits 0 while continuing to report the known story aggregate mismatch.

### 2155 — Review: mandatory Ed25519 and automatic-rekey rows lack positive adapter evidence (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- REVIEW: Changes requested after the round-10 ownership race was independently cleared: three consecutive `swift test --filter LibSSH2` runs pass 41 tests/3 suites, and the full core, pinned-source, tamper, Apple matrix, lint, diff, and board gates all exit 0.
- GAP: The revised M0 contract requires noninteractive Ed25519 plus an approved fallback through the opaque external signer. The live adapter fixture authenticates only with an in-memory P-256 signer; the pinned C rekey probe uses file-based Ed25519 auth and therefore does not exercise the adapter/Keychain signer boundary. A scoped search for an Ed25519 live-auth credential in `LibSSH2AdapterIntegrationTests.swift` exits 1.
- GAP: Client rekey requires byte-threshold, time-threshold, test, and manual triggers on the production KEX path under active traffic. Integration tests cover manual/test callers and server `RekeyLimit`, but configure the adapter byte threshold to `UInt64.max` and elapsed threshold to 3,600 seconds. A scoped search for `.byteThreshold`, `.timeThreshold`, `clientByteRekeys`, or `clientTimeRekeys` assertions in the adapter tests exits 1.
- REWORK: Add deterministic live adapter coverage for Ed25519 external signing and for both automatic rekey triggers under traffic, asserting KEX success, trigger counters/events, channel survival, bounded cancellation/close, and zero owned-resource baseline. If either path fails, fix the adapter rather than reporting the mandatory row green.

### 2144 — libssh2 upload timeout exposes deterministic ownership (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: The timeout race resumed its public continuation before cancelling the losing child, and the upload path returned after cancelling cooperative stdout/stderr work without waiting for those timeout-race records to retire. Depending on scheduling, the post-timeout snapshot contained the one intentionally non-cooperative upload source plus transient cooperative children; an unbounded test poll could then turn the ownership mismatch into a hung combined gate.
- REWORK: The race now signals cancellation before resuming its caller. The bounded ownership registry supports count-targeted retirement waiters, and a timed-out upload gives cooperative children at most 250 ms of the injected close budget to reconcile while permitting exactly one additional operation—the cancellation-ignoring source—to remain visible until release. The regression uses bounded monotonic state synchronization and fails instead of hanging if ownership cannot settle.
- VERIFICATION: The formerly order-sensitive `swift test --filter LibSSH2` gate passed three consecutive stress runs and a final post-edit run (41 tests/3 suites each). `make validate-core` passes 375 tests/31 suites plus boundaries and build; `make validate-libssh2`, `make test-libssh2-source-gates`, `make native-apple-matrix`, strict recursive Swift format, and `git diff --check` all exit 0. Board validation exits 0 while retaining the known `STORY-260715-lkshfz` aggregate mismatch as visible evidence.

### 2112 — libssh2 teardown reconciles reserved races and late automatic-task retirement (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: A reserved timeout race resumed teardown after its first child resolved but remained registered until both children retired. Teardown exposed completion without draining that registry, and it did not arrange durable automatic-task retirement until after socket close; if a cancellation-ignoring socket consumed the close deadline, the join branch was skipped and completed automatic-task references remained permanently counted.
- REWORK: Automatic-task retirement now starts before socket close and clears actor-owned references only after every cancelled task actually returns. The reserved registry is drained within the existing close deadline, so cooperative races reconcile before completion while genuinely non-cooperative socket work remains visible until its safe late return.
- CONFORMANCE: A connected OpenSSH regression starts with both automatic schedulers active, holds socket close past the 50 ms transport deadline while ignoring cancellation, proves bounded close, exact-once socket close, repeated-close safety, detached ownership visibility, and eventual zero sessions/channels/sockets/tasks/allocations/buffers after release. The formerly intermittent repeated-handshake cleanup passed 10 consecutive focused runs.
- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 41 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 375 tests/31 suites plus boundary checks and build; `make validate-libssh2`, `make test-libssh2-source-gates`, `make native-apple-matrix`, strict Swift format, `swift build`, and diff checks all exit 0. Board validation exits 0 with only the expected story aggregate mismatch while this task remains in development.

### 2053 — libssh2 socket teardown has capacity independent of ordinary operation pressure (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: Socket close used the same 64-slot timeout-race registry as ordinary injected and channel work. Teardown cancelled that registry and immediately tried to register close; while cancellation-ignoring children still occupied all slots, admission failed and `try?` skipped `SSHTCPConnection.close()` before dropping the connection reference.
- REWORK: Teardown now owns a separate fixed two-slot registry: one slot for the socket-close deadline and one for the automatic-task join deadline. Reserved admission is non-throwing under the single teardown owner, so ordinary saturation cannot bypass exact-once socket close; unfinished teardown children remain visible in the owned-resource snapshot until they retire.
- CONFORMANCE: A deterministic regression fills the ordinary registry to 64 while handshake readiness is suspended, starts close before releasing any operation, proves bounded public return and exactly one socket close, then releases late readiness and verifies no bridge read/write plus eventual zero ownership.
- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 40 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 374 tests/31 suites plus boundary checks and build; pinned libssh2/rekey and source-tamper gates, the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix, strict Swift format, and diff checks all exit 0. `task-board validate` exits 0 with the existing story aggregate mismatch (`to-dev` versus child `development`) still recorded rather than bypassed.

### 2032 — libssh2 teardown owns late sockets and bounds lifecycle reconciliation (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: Teardown established `transportClose` only after awaiting the injected socket close, automatic tasks, and every bridge service. Cooperative timeout-race children could still be visible when teardown returned, while a connector success arriving after its timeout was discarded without closing the returned socket.
- REWORK: The close deadline now starts at teardown entry. Socket close, automatic-task joins, bridge drain, and owned-operation reconciliation are bounded; cooperative children receive a short drain grace while genuinely non-cooperative work remains retained and counted. Timeout races accept an async abandoned-result disposer, and TCP connect uses it to close every late socket exactly once.
